  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "They didn't fold, but they never led either.",
    "what_this_means": "Detroit's two-run fifth inning opened the gap, and the Athletics couldn't close it. Jos\u00e9 Suarez kept them in it. One run allowed in 1.1 innings. But the offense mustered five hits and eleven strikeouts against a Tigers lineup that broke through once and held on. Jacob Wilson went 2-for-4, Jonah Heim chipped in, and they scored their one run on a RISP at-bat that mattered for nothing. The bullpen bridged the gap cleanly: Perkins threw three scoreless before Medina, Harris, and Leiter each contributed. Two outs in the ninth with runners on base. The game's last real chance. And the Athletics couldn't convert it. They left six on base across five RISP situations. One run doesn't survive when the division is six games away.",
    "what_to_watch": "This is a team that doesn't get behind and come back anymore. The pitching was good enough to win. The bullpen held. The offense couldn't meet either of them. Eleven strikeouts against a pitcher who'd thrown 1.1 innings is not a slump. It's a locked door. The sixth and ninth innings both had runners in scoring position. Both came up empty. The pattern isn't new: they score in bunches or they don't score at all. Tonight they were waiting for bunches that never came. Good pitching and empty at-bats is how stretches start.",
